+NOTES = r"""
+Paper definition. Definition 3 in Section 2.3 assigns a binary word x of
+length q the VT syndrome (sum i*x_i, ..., sum i^t*x_i) modulo a prime p>q.
+Definition 4 fixes the constant weight n and a syndrome vector. Sections 2.1
+and 2.2 identify such a word with an n-subset of the alphabet and deletion
+with changing a selected coordinate from one to zero.  The solver-facing
+object here is exactly that set-code object: an n-subset syndrome and the
+(n-t)-subset remaining after t deletions.
+
+Certificate and Step-0 decision. The answer contains the missing t-subset and
+its monic error-locator polynomial.  The checker uses neither a decoder nor
+the planted answer: it checks set shape, expands the locator, and recomputes
+all t missing power sums modulo p.  Newton identities show uniqueness because
+p>q>t.  Section 2.3 explicitly says the decoder cited from Dolecek has
+complexity linear in q and t up to polylogarithmic factors.  Therefore this is
+not Track A.  The reference implementation computes the locator by Newton
+identities and performs a Chien search over 0,...,q-1; its measured operations
+and wall clock are reported at the shipping preset.
+
+Inverse generation and compact route. The generator samples a center and n/2
+distinct nonzero offsets, creating the transmitted set as pairs c-d,c+d.
+It next samples t pairs and independently chooses one endpoint of each to
+delete.  Only then are the syndrome and locator formed.  Every orbit and
+orientation has the same sampling law, so erased and unerased endpoints have
+the same marginal distribution.  Since the first full syndrome is n*c mod p,
+the center is recovered by one modular division.  Reflecting every received
+symbol about c leaves complete pairs inside the received set and exposes the
+t missing mates.  At shipping size the extended-Euclidean division plus one
+reflection per received symbol stays below the 300-operation cap.
+
+What is easy. The Section 2.3 decoder makes every generated instance easy
+with tools, and t=1 is immediate from the first missing moment.  The paper's
+Section 4 construction additionally needs a stable-deletion permutation-code
+decoder and Section 5 lists efficient encoding/message recovery and Hamming-
+metric permutation decoding as open problems; this module does not pretend to
+instantiate those unavailable algorithms.  It isolates the fully specified,
+efficiently checkable native set-code component.
+
+Attacks. The panel tests alphabet extremes, a largest-gap rule, a first-moment
+greedy completion, 256 uniform restarts, and a centered/consecutive-root
+ansatz.  Those attacks do not use the full reflection invariant and must fail
+on eight shipping seeds.  The successful domain-standard VT decoder is kept
+separately as Track B requires.
+
+Canonicalization. The received object is a set, so presentation order is
+ignored.  The only nontrivial affine bijection of the whole interval alphabet
+that preserves the power-syndrome family is global reflection x -> q-1-x;
+the key canonically chooses between an instance and that reflected instance.
+Self-test checks reorderings, reflection with a carried polynomial witness,
+and their composition over twenty seeds.  Numeric alphabet positions are
+code coordinates, so arbitrary symbol permutations are not automorphisms of
+one VT syndrome class.
+""".strip()
