  1107	__all__ = [
  1108	    "TRACK", "PROBLEM_PROFILE", "NATIVE", "CERTIFICATE_LANGUAGE", "DIFFICULTY",
  1109	    "SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY", "STRUCTURAL_HINT", "PLACEBO_HINT", "NOTES",
  1110	    "make_instance", "render", "parse_answer", "verify", "random_candidate",
  1111	    "search_space", "enumerate_all", "canonical_key", "escalate", "selftest",
  1112	]
     1	# VT power-syndrome deletion decoding (arXiv:2501.13534)
     2	
     3	> Status: the generator and local gates G1--G8 are audited, but this result is not
     4	> shippable yet. OpenRouter quota reached zero during the one permitted medium-level
     5	> G9 rerun. `selftest_report.json` therefore records G9 as failing/pending rather
     6	> than claiming a passing result; this directory is not shippable yet.
     7	
     8	| profile field | value |
     9	|---|---|
    10	| Track | B -- no-tool compression |
    11	| Native domain | algebra |
    12	| Object regime | finite field |
    13	| Computational core | polynomial identity |
    14	| Certificate form | integer tuple (the paper's support set) |
    15	| Intended intuition | change of variables: detect an affine image of a centered template |
    16	| Domain essentiality | native |
    17	| Reduction | none |
    18	
    19	## Problem and trust model
    20	
    21	This family isolates the exact set-code decoder in Sections 2.1--2.3 of
    22	[Schaller, Toesca, and Vu, *A New Construction of Non-Binary Deletion Correcting
    23	Codes and their Decoding*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.13534). The solver receives
    24	the surviving support of a binary constant-weight word and its first `t` VT power
    25	sums in `F_p`, and must return the `t` deleted support positions. This is a native
    26	component of the paper's Construction 14/16 pipeline, not a graph encoding of it.
    27	
    28	`make_instance` samples the deleted support first as an affine image of a centered
    29	odd-step template, adds independently sampled survivors, and only then computes
    30	the syndrome. Thus generation is inverse generation, not decoding disguised as
    31	generation. `verify` checks shape, range, disjointness, and all power sums by exact
    32	modular arithmetic; it never reads `inst["answer"]`. Definition 4's asymmetric-error
    33	correction property gives uniqueness.
    34	
    35	## Why Track B
    36	
    37	Track A would be false. The paragraph following Definition 4 in Section 2.3 says
    38	the cited decoder is linear in the binary word length `q` and `t`, up to
    39	polylogarithmic factors. The bundled generic decoder instead uses Newton identities
    40	and Cantor--Zassenhaus factorization, with expected polynomial complexity in `t`
    41	and `log(p)`. At the medium preset it solved 8/8 cases in a median about 0.04 seconds
    42	and 437,032 counted exact operations.
    43	
    44	The compact route recognizes the deleted set as `c + uD`, recovers its two affine
    45	parameters from centered moments, and expands the fixed template. It takes 232
    46	counted exact operations at medium. That gap is the Track B claim: the generic route
    47	is easy with code but impossible to carry out in this no-tool context, while the
    48	compact route fits the 300-operation cap if the structure is noticed. The regimes
    49	deliberately avoided are `t=1` (one subtraction reveals the deletion), tiny fields
    50	(enumeration), and any claim that the paper's efficient decoder is structurally hard.
    51	
    52	## Worked demo
    53	
    54	The demo (`seed=5`) is genuinely hand-scale:
    55	
    56	```text
    57	VT POWER-SYNDROME DELETION DECODING
    58	
    59	Work in the prime field F_p with p = 47; every congruence below is modulo p.
    60	The alphabet positions are the ordinary integers 1 through q = 46, inclusive.
    61	A support is a set of distinct alphabet positions. It represents the 1-positions
    62	of a binary constant-weight word; a deletion changes a 1 to 0.
    63	
    64	The original support had exactly 6 positions. Exactly t = 4 positions were deleted.
    65	SURVIVORS 34 2
    66	SYNDROME 34 24 33 36
    67	
    68	Recover exactly 4 distinct positions in 1,...,46, disjoint from the survivors,
    69	in strictly increasing order, reproducing all four power sums modulo 47.
    70	Give: <answer>x1, x2, x3, x4</answer>
    71	```
    72	
    73	The answer is `<answer>11, 19, 27, 35</answer>`. Calling `verify` returns
    74	`(True, "ok")`; dropping 35 returns
    75	`(False, "expected exactly 4 deleted positions")`.
    76	
    77	## Difficulty presets
    78	
    79	| preset | requested `n` | actual `q` at seed 0 | `t` | survivors | status |
    80	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
    81	| demo | 43 | 46 | 4 | 2 | hand-solvable illustration |
    82	| easy | 5,000 | 5,002 | 48 | 2 | bare held, but structural hint solved 3/3 |
    83	| medium | 50,000 | 50,022 | 48 | 2 | intended shipping rung; G9 rerun blocked by quota |
    84	| hard | 200,000 | 200,002 | 48 | 2 | locally verified, not oracle-tested |
    85	
    86	## Local gate results
    87	
    88	| gate | result | measured evidence |
    89	|---|---|---|
    90	| G1 | pass | 12/12 preset/seed instances verified |
    91	| G2 | pass | five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
    92	| G3 | pass | tagged comma list and fenced JSON both round-trip |
    93	| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structured guesses; exact density `1.753793e-160` |
    94	| G5 | pass | unique shipping answer; demo exact count 1; generic decoder 439,740 operations on the fixed G5 seed |
    95	| G6 | pass | four attacks at 0/8; reference decoder 8/8 as Track B requires |
    96	| G7 | pass | doubling ambient size preserves a 48-element verified answer |
    97	| G8 | pass | 60 invariance and 60 witness-preservation checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
    98	| G9(c) | pass | at medium: 289-character bound, 97 lexical tokens, 48 atoms, 232 operations |
    99	| G9(b) | **blocked** | medium hinted run could not start after the API limit reached zero |
   100	
   101	The four failing G6 attacks were alternating small/large magnitudes, nearest-to-a-
   102	survivor greedy completion, 256 structure-aware random restarts, and the by-hand
   103	unit-scale ansatz. Plants and survivors have essentially uniform individual field
   104	marginals; only the planted points' joint affine relation carries signal.
   105	
   106	## Oracle evidence and G9 arms
   107	
   108	| run | preset | outcome | interpretation |
   109	|---|---|---|---|
   110	| bare | easy | 0 solved / 3 | prior bare run held |
   111	| hinted | easy | 3 solved / 3 | easy fails G9(b), so one move to medium is allowed |
   112	| bare | medium | 0 solved / 2 completed | incomplete: remaining calls returned quota errors |
   113	| hinted | medium | pending | required before shipping |
   114	| placebo | medium | pending | required diagnostic |
   115	
   116	No hinted-minus-placebo number is reported because the two medium arms are not
   117	complete. The easy result does show that the hint carries real structural
   118	information; it is not a mere prompt-length effect. After quota is restored, run
   119	all three arms in their existing separate scratch directories, copy the finished
   120	transcripts under the required `g9_*_transcript.jsonl` names, inject the counts into
   121	`G9_EVIDENCE`, and regenerate `selftest_report.json`.
   122	
   123	## Use
   124	
   125	```python
   126	import random
   127	import gen_2501_13534 as g
   128	
   129	inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY["medium"])
   130	statement = g.render(inst)
   131	candidate = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + ", ".join(map(str, inst["answer"])) + "</answer>")
   132	assert g.verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
   133	assert g.random_candidate(inst, random.Random(1)) != inst["answer"]  # overwhelmingly likely
   134	```
   135	
   136	Once G9 and the final bare run pass, emit from the repository root with
   137	`scripts/emit.sh 2501.13534`.
   138	
   139	## Caveats
   140	
   141	This benchmark covers the paper's VT set-code building block, not the permutation
   142	decoder or the complete multiplicity-free sequence decoder in Section 4. It becomes
   143	easy with any finite-field factorization package, for small `t`, or when the affine
   144	template is both disclosed and the modular arithmetic is executable. The G4 prior
   145	is uniform over distinct, survivor-disjoint, sorted `t`-sets already satisfying the
   146	first syndrome; its exact density says guessing from that declared language is
   147	hopeless, not that every conceivable inference attack is hopeless. I did not test
   148	Berlekamp factorization, higher-moment affine classifiers, or model access to a CAS;
   149	the successful generic factorizer already establishes that such tool-enabled routes
   150	are expected to work. The decisive unresolved caveat is medium G9(b), not hidden:
   151	the current OpenRouter key reports a total limit of 500 and zero remaining allowance.
