Implemented a verified Track B generator based on the paper’s VT power-syndrome decoder:

- [Generator module](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2501.13534/gen_2501_13534.py)
- [README and audit](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2501.13534/README.md)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2501.13534/selftest_report.json)
- [Existing oracle transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2501.13534/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)

The family uses inverse generation and exact finite-field verification. G1–G8 pass, including 0/200,000 structured guesses, four attacks at 0/8, and agreement between the planted answer, compact decoder, and generic factorization decoder across 120 additional cases.

It is not honestly shippable yet: G9 remains pending because the configured OpenRouter key reports a $500 total limit with zero remaining allowance. Easy bare held 0/3, but easy hinted solved 3/3; the permitted medium rerun completed only two bare failures before quota errors. Consequently:

- `G9_no_tool_suitability.pass` is correctly `false`.
- `all_passed` is correctly `false`.
- No missing G9 transcripts were fabricated.
- No `REJECTED.md` was written because this is an infrastructure block, not a failure of G, H, or V.

Once quota is restored, the prepared `g9_medium_bare`, `g9_medium_hinted`, and `g9_medium_placebo` directories can run the three required medium arms. The underlying construction follows Sections 2.1–2.3 of the [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.13534).
